A||||4||DJRice||Netscape and IE||||1
Z||000000||DJRice||11-24-1999||04:01 PM||||Becuase of the speed of my Network at Work, and my pathetic dial-up access at home, I normally surf the forum at work.  I use Netscape 4.7 at work and IE 5 at home.  I accessed the forum last night from home and decided to reply to a message.  It must have been the first time I replied from home since Cliff upgraded the software because I noticed something very cool.  <P>With Netscape, When you are replying to a message and would like to review the topic, a link is posted at the bottom of the page that will spawn a new window with the thread in it.<P>Internet explorer has a small scrolling window so that the window that you type the reply and the window that the original topic are in are visible in the same window so they wont dissappear back and forth.<P>Very Cool.  I just thought that I would post a note so that everyone would know what they might be missing.<P>On the other hand, I might be the only one who didnt know that the software would do that.||||reg||1
Z||000001||dogtired||11-24-1999||04:08 PM||||That is a great feature cause often in the middle of my reply I will forget<P><BR>What are we talking about?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000002||theogre||11-25-1999||12:42 AM||||actually IE originally used to give the link for a new window also. Cliff did some editing to change it to the framed display it is now. I was not aware that it was browser specific. I'm not sure if he knows that or not. <P>That's one of the hazards of web design. I used to try to test my stuff in multiple browsers, but I don't have time any more or disk space for that matter. I imagine Cliff has the same problem.<P>I do know that the server he's running shouldn't care, but the code he used might be off somehow. The fact he's gotten the "full" version running as well as it is has taken considerable time. ((I've talked to some people besides cliff about Ultimate. Cliff's not the only one who thinks it's a bit batty.))||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||11-25-1999||02:04 AM||||What's wrong with being a bit batty?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cliff Pennock||11-25-1999||03:46 AM||||I'm aware of the difference between the display of the "Review" in IE and Netscape. IE supports something called "in-line frames", while Netscape does not.<P>Ogre is right, it is very difficult trying to design webpages that will look the same in any browser. And to be honest, the only way to do it is to actually check what kind of browser a visitor is using and serve different HTML code for every browser.<P>What's <B>really</B> difficult is trying to make them look the same on different <I>platforms</I> (Win, Linux, Mac)...<BR>||||reg||1
